# Force LF line endings for scripts that run inside WSL2/Linux bash,
# regardless of a checking-out machine's own core.autocrlf setting.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: install.sh (LF in this repo) got checked out with CRLF
# line endings on a fresh Windows clone where core.autocrlf=true (the
# common Windows Git default) -- bash inside WSL2 then failed on every
# line with "$'\r': command not found" and a syntax error at EOF. This
# repo's own working copy didn't show the problem (files edited in place
# after an earlier clone, not freshly checked out), which is exactly the
# kind of machine-to-machine inconsistency a .gitattributes fixes: without
# it, whether a script works after `git clone` depends on the cloning
# machine's global git config, not on anything in this repo. Confirmed
# root cause via a real failure on a second machine (dheeraj's laptop),
# not assumed.
#
# .py and .ps1 files aren't currently failing the same way (Python
# tolerates CRLF fine, and .ps1 files only ever run on Windows where CRLF
# is native) but are pinned here too as a defensive default -- .py/.sh
# scripts specifically get executed as bash/python where CRLF can bite,
# and there's no reason for this repo's own history to carry mixed line
# endings depending on who last edited a file on which OS.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
